# SCADA / wind estate — SCALE-OUT BY TENANT. The rung is a SITE, not a rate.
#
# Every other workload here varies one dimension against a fixed population.
# An operator does not grow that way: they add a SITE, and a site arrives with
# its turbines, its message rate and its consumers all at once. Lane E is the
# only lane whose rung scales all three together, so it is the only one that
# can answer "how many sites fit on a cluster".
#
# The site is measured, not invented. From the estate this was sized against:
# 1,000 turbines per site, a few hundred tags each, averaged every 10s, which
# lands at ~30,000 msg/s per site at 200B. QoS 0 for 99% of it (telemetry to a
# datalake); the 1% that is alarms is QoS 1 and is NOT what this lane measures.
LANES=E
LANE_E_SITE_RATE=30000
LANE_E_QOS=0
LANE_E_PAYLOAD=200
# 1,200 publishers at 25 msg/s, not the nominal 1,000 at 30. emqtt-bench takes
# WHOLE milliseconds: 1,000 clients sharing 30,000 msg/s needs -I 33.33ms, and
# 33 offers 30,303 while 34 offers 29,412 — neither is the number on the label.
# 1,200 x 25 is exactly 40ms and exactly 30,000. The shape check refuses any
# combination that does not divide, so this cannot regress silently.
LANE_E_PUBS_PER_SITE=1200
# SIX consumers per site, which is a MEASURED choice and the main thing this
# file exists to record. ADR 0077 T3 originally specified two. The T7 probe
# (2026-08-28, QoS 0, $share of 10, one node) measured a consumer holding
# 5,000 msg/s at p99 <=1ms and 9,977 msg/s at p99 <=1000ms, then collapsing —
# 46,028 delivered of 200,000 offered at the next rung. Two consumers per site
# puts each at 15,000 msg/s, past everything sustained, and the FIRST rung
# would have failed for a reason that has nothing to do with tenancy.
#   6 consumers -> 5,000 msg/s each, comfortably inside that ceiling
#   3 consumers -> 10,000 msg/s each, at it
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-29 by lane E's own first run. The reasoning above justified
# six on the theory that per-consumer rate sets LATENCY. It does not. Placing
# lane E's rungs beside T7's, p99 tracks the broker's TOTAL and ignores the
# per-consumer rate almost entirely — three points at exactly 5,000 msg/s per
# consumer gave <=1ms, <=25ms and <=2000ms:
#
#    50,000 total / 10 consumers / 5,000 each -> <=1ms      (T7)
#    60,000 total / 12 consumers / 5,000 each -> <=25ms     (lane E, 2 sites)
#   120,000 total / 24 consumers / 5,000 each -> <=2000ms   (lane E, 4 sites)
#
# So consumer count is a DELIVERY-capacity choice, not a latency lever: pick
# enough to absorb the stream (>=3 per site) and adding more buys nothing.
# Latency comes from sites-per-node, and ultimately from #447 — the single hub
# thread was at 95% while the box was 77% busy. Six is kept because it leaves
# margin on the delivery side, not for the reason first written.
LANE_E_SUBS_PER_SITE=6
# Two publisher containers per site: one would ask a single emqtt-bench worker
# for the whole 30,000 msg/s, and the most any container on this rig has been
# measured to sustain is ~27,000 (2026-08-26, 25 containers offering 667k).
LANE_E_PUB_CONTAINERS_PER_SITE=2
LANE_E_SUB_CONTAINERS_PER_SITE=1
# The ladder doubles because the knee is the only point of interest. It stops
# at 8 because of the DRIVERS, not the broker: sites are dealt round-robin and
# each carries 3 one-vCPU containers, so 16 sites needs 12 containers on the
# busiest of 5 drivers against 8 vCPUs. The shape check refuses past that
# rather than letting a driver shortfall read as a broker limit — reaching 16
# needs DRIVER_COUNT=8, and 32 needs 16, which the project's server limit does
# not allow. That bound is the harness's and must be published as such.
LANE_E_SITES_OVERRIDE="1 2 4 8"
# FIVE drivers, not the shared default of two. With D=2 the 8-site rung needs
# 12 containers on the busiest driver and the shape check refuses the run — the
# ladder's reach is set by the driver count, so it belongs in this file next to
# the ladder it enables.
DRIVER_COUNT=5
# A rung passes only if p99 stays under this. The site count at which latency
# leaves the band is the sellable number; the count at which the broker finally
# refuses traffic is much higher and much less useful.
LANE_E_P99_BUDGET_MS=1000
